+# In-Memory Inversion of the Veterinarian–Specialty Association
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The specialty directory (`REQ-VET-003`) presents the veterinarian–specialty relation from the specialty side: each specialty, then the veterinarians holding it. The JPA mapping runs the other way. `Vet` owns an eager `@ManyToMany` over `Specialty` through the `vet_specialties` join table, and `Specialty` holds no collection of veterinarians. No code traverses the specialty-to-veterinarian direction today, and no repository exists for `Specialty`.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Inverse mapping** — add a `@ManyToMany(mappedBy = "specialties")` collection of veterinarians to `Specialty`, making the association bidirectional, and read the page from the specialty side.
+2. **Inverting query** — keep the mapping unidirectional and join `Specialty` to `Vet` in JPQL with an ad-hoc entity join, returning specialty–veterinarian tuples.
+3. **In-memory inversion** — read both sides with simple queries and pair them in a dedicated type.
+
+## Decision
+
+Option 3. A `SpecialtyRepository` returns every specialty in name order, the existing `VetRepository.findAll()` supplies the veterinarians with their specialties already loaded, and a `SpecialtyDirectory` value object pairs each specialty with the veterinarians holding it. The association stays unidirectional, and the new query uses the same explicit-`ORDER BY` form as `PetTypeRepository`.
+
+Option 1 makes `Specialty` reach back into `Vet`, which the `Vets` serialization wrapper walks — a cycle at serialization time — and widens a persisted type that both existing vet routes load, for the sake of one read-only page. Option 2 still returns rows that have to be grouped in memory, so it buys no work back while introducing a query construct that appears nowhere else in the codebase.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The inversion is a pure function over two collections. It is unit-testable with real `Vet` and `Specialty` instances and no Spring context, which is what [`testing-principles.md`](../testing-principles.md#mocking-policy) asks for.
+- The `Vet` and `Specialty` mappings are untouched, so the two existing vet routes and the `vets` cache behave exactly as before.
+- Every request for the directory materializes the whole veterinarian roster in memory. That is right at a clinic's scale and wrong for a large roster; moving the grouping into the query is the change to make if the roster grows.
+- Veterinarians come from the cached `findAll()`, so the directory shows whatever that cache holds. The specialty query is not cached — the `vets` cache is keyed by arguments alone, so a second no-argument method sharing that cache name would collide with `findAll()`.
+- `SpecialtyDirectory` is the first value object in this codebase; the *Value object* row of the pattern catalog was previously unrealized.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+Files: `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/vet/SpecialtyRepository.java`, `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/vet/SpecialtyDirectory.java`, `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/vet/VetController.java`.
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the types this decision adds
+- [Relational Persistence via Spring Data JPA](2026-07-31-relational-persistence-with-spring-data-jpa.md) — the mapping style this decision leaves unchanged
